{#
	What a module costs, drawn once so that three screens cannot disagree
	(XIV-102).

	**Only a priced module renders anything at all.** A free one is silent — not a
	"Free" badge, not a zero, nothing — and that is a decision rather than an
	oversight. Almost everything in this store is free and always will be for a
	deployment that sells nothing, so a badge on every tile would be noise on every
	tile: the absence of a price is the ordinary case and the ordinary case should
	look ordinary. It is also what makes this ticket invisible to an installation
	that has not priced anything, which is the acceptance criterion the whole
	feature was shaped around.

	The other two states never reach here. `unpriced` and `not_for_sale` are not
	offered in the store at all (§6.5), so this partial is only ever handed a
	module that is free or priced.

	**The number is drawn as it is stored**, a decimal string at two places with
	the ISO 4217 code beside it, and deliberately not through a locale-aware
	currency formatter. Three reasons, in increasing order of weight:

	  * `NumberFormatter::formatCurrency()` takes a float, and §5.9 is that nothing
	    on a money path is ever a float. This figure goes onto a purchase request
	    as an exact copy, so the value shown and the value stored have to be the
	    same string rather than the same number rounded twice.
	  * The currency may be unset, in which case there is nothing to format
	    *with* — see below.
	  * A customer who asks to buy something at `1'234.50` and later reads
	    `1234.50` on a request they made has to work out whether those are the same
	    figure. On a customer's own invoice a formatted amount is right; on a price
	    somebody is about to commit to, the stored value is.

	**An unset currency shows a bare number, and says nothing about why.** §8.6
	refuses to guess a currency for a customer because a guessed one is wrong
	quietly, and the same holds for a price list — so a deployment that has never
	set `PRICE_CURRENCY` prices in nothing and the figure stands alone. The
	operator screen names the variable in that situation, because an operator can
	go and set it; this screen does not, because a customer cannot, and telling
	them about an environment variable on somebody else's server would be leaking a
	deployment detail in place of an answer. §6.5 settled the same trade one page
	over.
#}
{% if offer.costsMoney %}
	<span class="{{ emphasis|default('fw-semibold') }}">
		<span class="font-monospace">{{ offer.price.amount }}</span>
		{%- if currency %} <span class="text-body-secondary">{{ currency }}</span>{% endif -%}
	</span>
{% endif %}
